  <section class="acdt-intro content-section" aria-label="Quick Guide Summary">
    <div class="info-callout speakable-summary">
      <h3>Quick Summary: What Are Time Zone Abbreviations?</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Time zone abbreviations are short alphabetical codes (usually three to five letters) representing specific local time offsets relative to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC).</strong> 
        For instance, Eastern Standard Time is abbreviated as **EST** (UTC-5), Greenwich Mean Time as **GMT** (UTC+0), and Central European Summer Time as **CEST** (UTC+2). 
        Because these codes are created regionally rather than by a single international authority, some acronyms are identical across different continents. 
        For example, **IST** represents Indian Standard Time (UTC+5:30), Irish Standard Time (UTC+1 in summer), and Israel Standard Time (UTC+2).
      </p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="reading-meta" style="margin-top: 1rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
      <span>⏱️ Estimated Reading Time: <strong>32 minutes</strong></span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
