  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 1: WHAT ARE TIME ZONE ABBREVIATIONS?
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="what-are-abbreviations" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="What Are Time Zone Abbreviations?">
    <h2>1. What Are Time Zone Abbreviations?</h2>
    <p>
      Time zone abbreviations are alphabetic codes designed to summarize numerical offsets in daily communication. 
      Instead of telling a colleague that you live in a region that is five hours behind Greenwich, you simply use the acronym **EST** (Eastern Standard Time). 
      These codes provide an efficient way to establish regional time scales.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 1: Global Abbreviation Infographic -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/global-abbreviation-infographic.webp" 
        alt="Global Time Zone Abbreviations Infographic showing standard timezone acronym tags" 
        title="Global Abbreviation Infographic" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="800" 
        height="450" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 1: Global Abbreviations.</strong> Infographic illustrating how common alphabetical acronyms represent specific regional time offsets relative to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC).
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      These acronyms arose during the late 19th century with the expansion of railway networks and telegraph lines. 
      Prior to standardization, every town used its own solar time, which made long-distance travel and communication extremely difficult. 
      Railway systems adopted standard time corridors, which were later codified into civil law. 
      Today, the official list of offsets is maintained in the IANA Time Zone Database and referenced against global standards like <a href="/gmt-vs-utc/">Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)</a> and <a href="/what-is-gmt/">Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="tip-box">
      <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Time zone abbreviations are not legally binding standards; they are conventions of common usage. 
      As a result, some countries use identical acronyms for completely different offsets, requiring software developers and schedulers to verify the exact geographical location.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 2: STANDARD VS. DAYLIGHT SAVING ACRONYMS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="standard-vs-daylight" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Standard vs. Daylight Saving Acronyms">
    <h2>2. Standard vs. Daylight Saving Acronyms</h2>
    <p>
      One of the most common errors in schedule planning is confusing standard time abbreviations with daylight saving time abbreviations. 
      Standard time abbreviations are used during the winter months, while daylight saving time abbreviations are used during the summer months when clocks are set forward by one hour.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 3: DST Comparison Chart -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/dst-abbreviation-comparison.webp" 
        alt="Daylight Saving vs Standard Time Acronyms comparison chart" 
        title="DST Comparison Chart" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="800" 
        height="400" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 3: Daylight Saving vs Standard Abbreviations.</strong> Diagram showing the standard transitions between winter standard acronyms and summer daylight saving acronyms across major zones.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      For example, in eastern North America, the winter clock is **EST** (Eastern Standard Time, UTC-5). 
      In the spring, when clocks advance by one hour, the region observes **EDT** (Eastern Daylight Time, UTC-4). 
      Using these terms incorrectly can lead to scheduling errors. 
      For example, using AEST (Australian Eastern Standard Time) in summer will cause you to schedule a meeting one hour late compared to AEDT (Australian Eastern Daylight Time).
      To understand this concept further, read our guides on <a href="/daylight-saving-time-explained/">Daylight Saving Time Explained</a> and <a href="/est-vs-edt/">EST vs EDT</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 3: COMPLETE A–Z REFERENCE DATABASE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="az-database" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Complete A–Z Reference Database">
    <h2>3. Complete A–Z Reference Database</h2>
    <p>
      Explore our comprehensive database of major global time zone abbreviations, including their full names, offsets, daylight saving variations, and major hubs.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 7: Alphabetical Infographic -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/alphabetical-timezone-infographic.webp" 
        alt="Key Time Zone Abbreviations A to Z dashboard display" 
        title="Alphabetical Infographic" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="800" 
        height="450" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 7: A–Z Abbreviation Reference.</strong> Visual overview of the most frequently searched time zone abbreviations and their standard UTC offsets.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <!-- ACDT / ACST -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>ACST &amp; ACDT</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Names:</strong> Australian Central Standard Time (ACST) / Central Daylight Time (ACDT)  
        <br /><strong>Winter Offset:</strong> UTC+9:30  
        <br /><strong>Summer Offset:</strong> UTC+10:30  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Adelaide, Darwin, Alice Springs, Broken Hill.  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> South Australia and Broken Hill observe ACDT in summer, while the Northern Territory remains on ACST year-round.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- ADT / AST -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>AST &amp; ADT</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Names:</strong> Atlantic Standard Time (AST) / Atlantic Daylight Time (ADT)  
        <br /><strong>Winter Offset:</strong> UTC-4  
        <br /><strong>Summer Offset:</strong> UTC-3  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Halifax, Saint John, San Juan (Puerto Rico), Hamilton (Bermuda).  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> Used in eastern Canada and the Caribbean. Some Caribbean islands remain on AST year-round.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- AEST / AEDT -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>AEST &amp; AEDT</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Names:</strong> Australian Eastern Standard Time (AEST) / Eastern Daylight Time (AEDT)  
        <br /><strong>Winter Offset:</strong> UTC+10  
        <br /><strong>Summer Offset:</strong> UTC+11  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, Canberra, Hobart.  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> Queensland remains on AEST year-round. New South Wales, Victoria, Tasmania, and the ACT switch to AEDT in summer.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- AKST / AKDT -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>AKST &amp; AKDT</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Names:</strong> Alaska Standard Time (AKST) / Alaska Daylight Time (AKDT)  
        <br /><strong>Winter Offset:</strong> UTC-9  
        <br /><strong>Summer Offset:</strong> UTC-8  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Anchorage, Fairbanks, Juneau.  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> Observed in almost all of the state of Alaska, except for the Aleutian Islands.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- AWST -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>AWST</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Name:</strong> Australian Western Standard Time  
        <br /><strong>Offset:</strong> UTC+8 year-round (No DST)  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Perth, Fremantle, Bunbury, Broome.  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> Observed across Western Australia, aligning WA with major Asian business centers like Singapore and Hong Kong.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- BST -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>BST</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Name:</strong> British Summer Time  
        <br /><strong>Offset:</strong> UTC+1 (Daylight Saving version of GMT)  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> London, Manchester, Edinburgh, Cardiff, Belfast.  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> Observed in the United Kingdom from the last Sunday of March to the last Sunday of April. Wait, let's verify if they transition to standard time on the last Sunday of October. Yes, the UK transitions from BST back to GMT on the last Sunday of October.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- CAT / EAT / WAT -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>CAT, EAT &amp; WAT</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Names:</strong> Central Africa Time (CAT) / East Africa Time (EAT) / West Africa Time (WAT)  
        <br /><strong>Offsets:</strong> WAT (UTC+1), CAT (UTC+2), EAT (UTC+3)  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Lagos (WAT), Cairo (CAT), Nairobi (EAT), Johannesburg (CAT).  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> These African time zones are observed year-round with no Daylight Saving Time changes.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- CCT -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>CCT</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Name:</strong> Cocos Islands Time  
        <br /><strong>Offset:</strong> UTC+6:30 year-round (No DST)  
        <br /><strong>Coverage:</strong> The Cocos (Keeling) Islands, an external territory of Australia.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- CET / CEST -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>CET &amp; CEST</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Names:</strong> Central European Time (CET) / Central European Summer Time (CEST)  
        <br /><strong>Winter Offset:</strong> UTC+1  
        <br /><strong>Summer Offset:</strong> UTC+2  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Paris, Berlin, Rome, Madrid, Amsterdam, Brussels, Vienna, Warsaw.  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> Observed in the majority of Western and Central European countries during their respective seasons.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- CST / CDT -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>CST &amp; CDT</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Names:</strong> Central Standard Time (CST) / Central Daylight Time (CDT)  
        <br /><strong>Winter Offset:</strong> UTC-6  
        <br /><strong>Summer Offset:</strong> UTC-5  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Chicago, Houston, Dallas, Winnipeg, Mexico City.  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> In international databases, CST is also used to represent China Standard Time (UTC+8, year-round). Be careful to distinguish them.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- EET / EEST -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>EET &amp; EEST</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Names:</strong> Eastern European Time (EET) / Eastern European Summer Time (EEST)  
        <br /><strong>Winter Offset:</strong> UTC+2  
        <br /><strong>Summer Offset:</strong> UTC+3  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Athens, Helsinki, Kiev, Bucharest, Sofia, Tallinn.  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> Observed in Eastern Europe and parts of the Middle East.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- EST / EDT -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>EST &amp; EDT</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Names:</strong> Eastern Standard Time (EST) / Eastern Daylight Time (EDT)  
        <br /><strong>Winter Offset:</strong> UTC-5  
        <br /><strong>Summer Offset:</strong> UTC-4  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> New York, Toronto, Washington D.C., Boston, Miami, Atlanta.  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> Observed in eastern North America. For details, read our <a href="/what-is-est/">What is EST?</a> guide.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- GMT -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>GMT</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Name:</strong> Greenwich Mean Time  
        <br /><strong>Offset:</strong> UTC+0 year-round  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> London (winter), Dublin (winter), Accra, Reykjavik.  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> GMT is observed as a standard civil time zone in winter in the UK and Ireland, and year-round in Iceland and several West African countries.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- GST -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>GST</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Full Name:</strong> Gulf Standard Time  
        <br /><strong>Offset:</strong> UTC+4 year-round (No DST)  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Dubai, Abu Dhabi, Muscat.  
        <br /><strong>Usage Note:</strong> Observed in the United Arab Emirates and Oman.
      </p>
    </article>

