  <section id="programming-guidelines" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Programming &amp; Technology Guidelines">
    <h2>6. Programming &amp; Technology Guidelines</h2>
    <p>
      In software engineering, relying on timezone abbreviations inside databases or network APIs is considered a critical error. 
      Acronyms are ambiguous and do not correspond to unique numerical offsets.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 8: Programming Illustration -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/programming-timezone-abbreviations.webp" 
        alt="Developers Timezone Reference Pipeline diagram" 
        title="Programming Illustration" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="800" 
        height="400" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 8: Developer Timezone Pipeline.</strong> Diagram showing the standard database pipeline: storing time in UTC and formatting to local abbreviations at the client rendering layer using IANA location databases.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>1. Never Store Abbreviations in Databases</h3>
    <p>
      Do not store dates as "2026-07-15 10:00:00 EST". 
      The acronym "EST" can map to Eastern Standard Time (UTC-5) or Australian Eastern Standard Time (AEST/EST, UTC+10) depending on server environment settings. 
      Always store timestamps in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) using the ISO 8601 formatting: `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Use IANA Identifiers</h3>
    <p>
      Use the official IANA location identifiers (like `America/New_York` or `Europe/Paris`) to format UTC dates into localized abbreviations. 
      These databases contain complete historical records of DST changes and local law changes.
    </p>

    <h3>3. JavaScript Formatter Example</h3>
    <p>
      To display a UTC date as a localized abbreviation in the user interface, utilize the `Intl.DateTimeFormat` API:
    </p>

    <pre style="background: var(--panel); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--line); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text); font-family: monospace;">{`const date = new Date('2026-07-15T12:00:00Z');
const formatter = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', {
  timeZone: 'America/New_York',
  timeZoneName: 'short'
});
console.log(formatter.format(date)); 
// Output will render with EDT/EST abbreviation based on the date`}</pre>
  </section>

