  <section id="common-pitfalls" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Timezone Acronym Pitfalls">
    <h2>7. Common Timezone Acronym Pitfalls</h2>
    <p>
      Avoid these frequent errors when managing global travel, itineraries, or calendar schedules:
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 9: Travel Planning Illustration -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/travel-timezone-abbreviations.webp" 
        alt="Travel Itinerary Timezone Transitions showing PDT to BST flight details" 
        title="Travel Planning Illustration" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="800" 
        height="400" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 9: Travel Timezone Planning.</strong> Boarding card and timeline details illustrating the importance of checking local offsets during transcontinental travel.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>The IST Confusion:</strong> If a client in Dublin says "let's meet at 10:00 a.m. IST," they may be referring to Irish Standard Time (UTC+1 in summer). 
        If a team member in New Delhi assumes Indian Standard Time (UTC+5:30), they will miss the meeting by 4.5 hours. 
        Always clarify the country.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Assuming All Regions Observe DST:</strong> Not all states in North America observe daylight saving. 
        Arizona remains on MST (UTC-7) year-round, meaning it does not transition to MDT. 
        Ensure your itineraries reflect these local exceptions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The CST Ambiguity:</strong> CST represents Central Standard Time (UTC-6) in North America, China Standard Time (UTC+8) in Asia, and Cuba Standard Time (UTC-5) in the Caribbean. 
        Always verify the geographical context.
      </li>
    </ol>

